  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "R5, R8, and R18 already describe temperature-dependent molecular-dynamics deformation of the closely related equiatomic CoNiCrFeMn FCC alloy in nanowire form, including partial-dislocation motion, stacking-fault/FCC-HCP activity, and associated strengthening. R4 and R13 additionally establish high-rate, temperature-dependent MD tensile loading in related HEAs, while R3, R12, and R16 connect temperature-dependent deformation in bulk Cantor-type alloys to dislocation processes. Thus, the records motivate extending tensile MD to a related FeNiCrMn FCC crystal and examining temperature, strain rate, and dislocation-mediated plasticity. However, they do not specifically supply the bulk quaternary model, the three-direction crystallographic comparison, the particular strain-rate sweep, or vacancy-cluster-controlled nucleation. The closest nanowire studies also emphasize free-surface nucleation, twinning, reorientation, and FCC-HCP transformation, whereas the later result finds vacancy-site nucleation, local stacking-fault-induced HCP character, and no twinning or bulk transformation. Those differences require a substantial additional idea, so the result is only partially reconstructible."
}
